A few days ago I ascended in PC Nethack 3.1.3 for the first time, after
over a month of fairly intensive playing. I was playing a Tourist (of
all things!) and ascended in 63,224 moves with 3,260,710 points and
was 23rd level with 208 hp. Pretty crappy, I know, but I was mainly
concentrating on winning the game for the first time rather than
accumulating loot. I did it without restoring any saved games (in fact
I died 0 times) or wishing for artifacts but I did polypile and
polymorph myself. I've never read the spoiler file or looked at the
code, all I know about the game I got from playing and (mainly) reading
this newsgroup, so thanks!

Anyway, for people who like reading boring descriptions of ascensions,
I will now record for posterity basically how it went.
(Since the following describes as example of completing the game, it
 probably contains spoilers...)






















Things started off pretty normally until I got to the Gnomish Mine Town,
where due to my crappy weapon and character I became beseiged in the
temple by a hoard of tough monsters. I spent ages gradually picking
them off from the doorway [didn't get anything from sacrificing them,
though :(] until I thought they had thinned out enough
to make a dash for the stairs down, and made it. Things got complicated
again a couple of levels further down and this time I had no temple to
hide in. I was getting pretty desperate and was being hedged into a
dead end by a vicious bugbear I couldn't seem to kill (boy did I have
a useless weapon, forget what it was though) when I decided the only
chance of escape I had was to zap myself with my recently acquired
wand of polymorph. Zap! Meow! Not good. I zap myself again but merely
shudder and take heaps of damage, leaving me on 1 hp! By now, the
bugbear is right next to me and I am already thinking of whether or
not I want my items identified as I resignedly zap myself again.
What luck! I turned into a blue dragon! I quickly dispensed with
the bugbear and immensely enjoyed returning to the Town and cleaning
it out of my former tormentors. It still gives me a warm glow when I
think about how I came back from being a 1 hp housecat cowering before
a murderous bugbear to win the game!
I soon returned to the Dungeons of Doom rather optimistically equipped
with some candles (this may be the largest parenthesized passage in
history, but not long before this character I'd played another Tourist
who was my first character to get into Gehennom, and in fact he
eventually got his hands on the real Amulet. However, up until that
point I had been assuming that my games would not reach such dizzying
heights and _had not been buying candles_ as a rule. So I got to the
vibrating square with everything but some candles. What to do? Why,
go back to the Gnomish Mines and get some, of course! Needless to say,
the Wizard eventually got that character, but not before I'd made it
back, recovered the amulet and killed him 7 times) now where was I ah
yes rather optimistically equipped with some candles and found a like
aligned altar on about level 12. I finally got a decent weapon
(Giantslayer) from this altar and from there it was a pretty routine
journey to the castle (on the way, however, I found my first Wand of Wishing
in a shop). I'd pretty much cleaned out the castle when I went
up to 14th level and returned to do the Quest. In one of the shops in
the Quest I found Demonbane. Then it was time to begin the final descent
into the underworld. The Valley of the Dead was very fruitful, yielding
not only Mjollnir but yet another Wand of Wishing (3 now!). Unfortunately,
one of the traps there wiped out my ring of fire resistance and levitation,
so I wasted a wish getting a ring of fire resistance (I'm a newbie, alright?)
I delayed getting back the ring of levitation and was eventually able
to polypile one before I needed it again, as well as en extra amulet of
life saving. I made a fairly standard trip through Gehennom, mapping and
digging between each set of stairs down to the vibrating square, unlike
my aforementioned Tourist who simply dropped with wands of digging.
On the way I dispatched Asmodeus and Juiblex, met a peaceful(?) Orcus who
I "let live", and had a clumsy horned devil with a wand of death do the
job for me on Baalzebub ("Oops! Sorry boss!"). Being dragged back down
levels with the Amulet became a bit of a problem, especially around
the rather convoluted levels of Asmodeus and Juiblex which were either
consecutive or had only one level between them, but eventually I escaped
Gehennom. Here I decided to try the "potion of paralysis" trick on the
Wizard that someone posted a while ago, and it worked! I paralysed him
non-adjacent to the stairs, went up, and never saw him again! This is
a bit of a bug, I don't think it should be allowed to happen.
The planes of Earth and Air were pretty hard (I got down to my lowest
level of about 20 hp on Air) but being fire resistant I managed to hide
in fire vortices and heal up on Fire.  I'd run out of confusion potions
and detect treasure scrolls by halfway through the endgame and was
having trouble finding the portals, so I scribed some detect treasure
scrolls and, seeing I had a wish or to left, _wished for 3 potions of
confusion_. Wow! Has anyone seriously made a weaker wish than this?
That's about it really. As a final note, my ring of conflict helped me
immeasurably throughout the second 2/3 of the game, I think it's
defenitely one of the better items to have. Well, that was "How I made
my first ascension" by T.Wedd, hope you enjoyed it.

